course: dictionary
lesson: 15386
title: Specs: one short word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some short words have more than one meaning. Context, the words around them, tells you which meaning is right. «Specs» is an informal short word with two different meanings.
explain: «Where are my specs? I can't read the sign without them.» Here specs means glasses. You wear glasses to see better, and you need them to read the sign.
explain: «Dad compared the specs of the two laptops: the faster one had more memory.» Here specs means specifications, a detailed description of what a machine can do. Ask: would glasses fit this sentence? No, so the meaning must be specifications.
fact: Specs is informal, so you will hear it in everyday talk more than in formal writing.
fact: «Specs» can mean glasses that help people see better.
fact: «Specs» can also mean specifications, a detailed description of how something is made or what it can do.
fact: Specs is a short form of two longer words: spectacles and specifications.
fact: Phrases to notice: «reading specs» (glasses for reading) and «high specs» (very good features in a computer or phone).
exercise: «Grandad can't find his specs, so he can't read the menu.» What are specs here? | A detailed list of how something is made | A kind of pet | *Glasses worn to help you see | A type of football shirt
exercise: «Check the specs before you buy the new phone: look at the memory and screen size.» What does specs mean here? | A shop assistant | A phone case | *A detailed list of what the phone can do | A pair of glasses
exercise: In «This gaming console has high specs», what does specs mean? | Special shoes for gaming | A type of game | Glasses for seeing the screen | *Detailed features showing how powerful the console is
test: «Put on your specs and read the small print.» What clue tells you specs means glasses? | *Put on, which is what you do with glasses | Read, which is only done with a phone | The word your, which is always glasses | Small print, which is always on glasses
test: Ella says specs always means glasses. Is she right? | No, specs means the same as spectacles only in Scotland | No, specs is not a real word | Yes, specs always means glasses | *No, specs can also mean specifications, so the sentence must be checked
exam: Tom says, «I'm buying a new tablet, so I'm checking its specs.» What does specs mean here? | Glasses that help him see the screen | A cheap tablet case | The shop where he buys it | *Specifications, the detailed description of what the tablet can do
